WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
We just built a tool that lets us talk to our data.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.600
Anyone on our team can ask a question like,

3
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.280
which geographies tend to convert the most leads?

4
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.400
Or how are bookings trending week over week?

5
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.040
And it'll instantly generate the answer for them.

6
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.320
It can make charts and visuals on the fly,

7
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.840
and we can even use it to start making predictions

8
00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.960
based on our historical data.

9
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.400
So when ChatGPT first came out,

10
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.880
this was one of those use cases

11
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.360
that everybody immediately jumped to.

12
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:29.000
And it actually worked pretty well

13
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.720
as long as you were working with one relatively simple dataset.

14
00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:36.280
The problem comes when you need to start connecting different datasets,

15
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:39.280
which is what pretty much every company needs to do.

16
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.040
That's because, for instance, in your CRM,

17
00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:46.080
a customer might be referred to by account_id.

18
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.440
And in your finance system,

19
00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.160
that same customer might be called the sold_to_party.

20
00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:55.320
It gets even messier when you realize that marketing and finance

21
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.920
might have completely different definitions of something like revenue,

22
00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.280
or what counts as an active customer.

23
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.520
The reason a vendor or off-the-shelf tool can't just fix this automatically

24
00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:08.920
is that your organization has spent years accumulating knowledge

25
00:01:08.920 --> 00:01:11.400
about how to interpret its own data.

26
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:14.520
That's implicit rather than explicit context,

27
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:18.760
and no tool can understand it unless you teach it how to understand it.

28
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.920
So we discovered that what we actually needed

29
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.280
is something called a semantic layer.

30
00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.360
Basically, it's a way of teaching the AI what all of our terms mean,

31
00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:29.760
how the different datasets relate to each other,

32
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.000
and what rules our business uses to calculate things.

33
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.160
That's why building natural language analytics

34
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.880
took a lot more effort than we first thought it would.

35
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:41.640
We needed the people who keep all of this implicit context in their heads

36
00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.040
to sit down and define all of it for the machines.

37
00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.200
The good news is, now that we've done it,

38
00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:51.200
we have a composable knowledge layer that any AI tool can tap into.
